<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad ibn Qasim</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">695–<a href="/years/715">715 CE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad ibn Qasim was an Umayyad general who conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sind">Sind</a> in the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> valley around 711 to 712. His campaign brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and Umayyad rule to part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a>. It was one of the eastern limits of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a>'s expansion.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-qasim.jpg" alt="Muhammad ibn Qasim" width="320" height="157" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG Coins · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Umayyad_Caliphate_coinage_temp_Suleiman_ibn_Abd_al-Malik_al-Hind_(possibly_Multan)_mint._Dated_AH_97_(AD_715-6).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Muhammad ibn al-Qasim al-Thaqafi belonged to the Banu Thaqif, an Arab tribe centred on the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taif">Taif</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>. His first assignment was in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>, where he subdued a group of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurds">Kurds</a> and was appointed governor, probably succeeding his uncle Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Thaqafi.</p><p>Umayyad interest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> was stirred by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>, among them the Meds, who preyed on shipping from bases at Kutch, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/debal">Debal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Kathiawar</a>. Muslim sources say that the kidnapping of Muslim women travelling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> by the Meds of Debal gave the pretext, and that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raja-dahir">Raja Dahir</a>, the ruler, refused to release prisoners or pay compensation, saying that he had no control over the raiders.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-hajjaj-ibn-yusuf">Al-Hajjaj ibn Yusuf</a>, the governor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, sent Muhammad against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> in 711, when he was in his teens, after two earlier commanders had failed. The army that left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> held 6,000 Syrian cavalry and detachments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mawali">mawali</a>, joined by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a> cavalry and by reinforcements and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catapult">catapults</a> sent by sea to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/debal">Debal</a>, at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a>. Towns were given the choice of submitting peacefully or being attacked. Where they were taken by force, fighting men were executed and surviving dependants enslaved.</p><p>As governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> from 712 until his death he asked for acceptance of Muslim rule in return for non-interference in religious practice, so long as taxes and tribute were paid. Villages kept their headmen and their own laws, non-Muslims paid the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jizya">jizya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> and Buddhists were taken into the administration.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Al-Hajjaj died in 714 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-walid-i">al-Walid I</a> a year later. The new caliph, Sulayman, took revenge on the generals close to al-Hajjaj. Muhammad was put in chains and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a> on 18 July 715. Al-Baladhuri says that he was tortured to death by officials who had a grievance against al-Hajjaj's family; some sources place his burial in Makran. Dahir's son soon recaptured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmanabad">Brahmanabad</a>.</p><p>Historians disagree about the conquest. Stanley Lane-Poole called it liberal, while early historians such as Elliot and Majumdar held that the conversion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> was coercive. Others, among them Thomas W. Arnold, argue that it was largely peaceful. Conversion was in fact slow, and most of the population stayed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>. Port Qasim is named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muhammad ibn al-Qasim” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Indus plain held Mehrgarh, the cities of the Indus Valley Civilisation and Gandhara, and official chronology dates the foundation of Pakistan to <b>Muhammad ibn Qasim</b>'s conquest of Sindh in 711.</blockquote><a href="/empire/islamic-republic-of-pakistan">Islamic Republic of Pakistan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the east, Qutayba ibn Muslim conquered Bukhara and Samarkand in Transoxiana, and <b>Muhammad ibn Qasim</b> led the Umayyad conquest of Sind in 711–712.</blockquote><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After attacks on Arab ships near Debal, the Umayyad governor of Iraq, al-Hajjaj, sent <b>Muhammad ibn Qasim</b>, who took Debal in 711 and defeated and killed Dahir in 712.</blockquote><a href="/state/brahman-dynasty-of-sindh">Brahman dynasty of Sindh</a></li><li><blockquote>Multan was conquered by <b>Muhammad ibn Qasim</b> in 713.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-multan">Emirate of Multan</a></li><li><blockquote>Sindh had been an Umayyad and then Abbasid province since the conquest by <b>Muhammad ibn Qasim</b> in 711 to 712.</blockquote><a href="/state/habbari-dynasty">Habbari dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taif">Ta'if</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=21.27,40.42&amp;year=708" aria-label="Ta'if on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5636 1377 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Muhammad ibn Qasim"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6086 1647h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6086 1647h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=21.27,40.42&amp;year=708">What was here in 708 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
